WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.120
Ben Wattenberg: Hello. I’m Ben Wattenberg.
In the summer of 1969, hundreds of thousands

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:16.080
of young Americans converged on a farm in
upstate New York for the now-legendary Woodstock

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.349
Music and Arts Fair. Did Woodstock mark the
end of the ’60s, as some say, or was it

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:27.390
the beginning of a countercultural revolution
that is still playing itself out in American

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.390
society?

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:34.150
Joining us to sort through the conflict and
consensus are Kurt Vonnegut, novelist and

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:38.309
social critic and author of more than a score
of books, including “Slaughterhouse Five,”

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:44.170
“Cat’s Cradle,” and “Breakfast of
Champions”; Martha Bayles, author of “Hole

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.299
in Our Soul: The Loss of Beauty and Meaning
in American Popular Music” and an editorial

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:54.299
consultant for our program; Morris Dickstein,
professor of English at the Graduate Center

00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:59.110
of the City University of New York and author
of “The Gates of Eden: American Culture

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:04.129
in the Sixties”; and Jim Miller, professor
of political science at the New School for

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.370
Social Research and author of “Democracy
Is in the Streets: From Port Huron Through

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.950
the Siege of Chicago.”

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:33.990
The question before this house: Did Woodstock
change America? This week on “Think Tank.”

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:40.950
In 1969, close to a half a million self-described
hippies, flower children, and Aquarians made

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:53.929
a pilgrimage to Woodstock in upstate New York.
The Woodstock Music and Arts Festival was

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:59.630
called an Aquarian exposition and hailed as
three days of peace and music. And it was

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.439
peaceful.

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:07.350
But the times were not. America seemed to
be dividing. The war in Vietnam went on and

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:12.660
on. More American boys were still being sent
to Southeast Asia by newly elected President

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:19.700
Nixon. The increasingly unpopular war intensified
a campus protest movement. Burning draft cards

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.440
and peace marches became daily fare on the
evening news.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.340
And not all the demonstrations were peaceful.
The previous summer after Martin Luther King

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:35.250
was assassinated, riots broke out in 125 cities.
And at the Democratic convention in Chicago,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.600
the anti-war movement tore the Democratic
Party in two.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:45.890
Both critics and fans concede that Woodstock
has become part of the mythology of the 1960s,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.430
even if the actual event didn’t necessarily
represent the musical or political taste of

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:56.330
most young Americans at the time. Some say
it symbolized the freedom and idealism of

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:02.550
the 1960s. Critics argue that Woodstock represented
much of what was wrong with the ’60s: a

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:08.570
glorification of drugs, a loosening of sexual
morality, and a socially corrosive disrespect

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.290
for authority.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.440
Twenty-five years later, the debate still
rages about the meaning of Woodstock and,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:26.070
more importantly, about the legacy of those
who attended it in person or in spirit.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:35.450
Martha Bayles, welcome — welcome to you
all. What happened at Woodstock? Is Woodstock

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.130
a legitimate symbol of the ’60s, of the
counterculture?

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:44.360
Martha Bayles: I think Woodstock is laden
with a lot of symbolic meaning, and that’s

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.600
why we’re arguing about it and we still
talk about it. But the meanings are numerous;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.670
Woodstock was a mixed bag.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:58.830
To the hardcore left in 1969, Woodstock was
nothing but a rock concert. It wasn’t a

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.240
demonstration, and a lot of people criticized
it on that basis.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.890
Nowadays when Woodstock is being compared
to the highly commercialized Woodstock ’94,

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:13.630
it’s seen as a sort of pure, spontaneous
ingathering of free spirits practicing peace

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:18.259
and love, when in fact it was a commercial
venture. It was a rock concert, and without

00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:22.379
the profit motive and the presence of popular
recording stars, it probably wouldn’t have

00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:27.699
happened anyway. But there were some very
positive things at Woodstock, things that

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:30.050
are positive about the ’60s, and some negative
things.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:34.930
Ben Wattenberg: We’re going to come back
to that. Jim Miller, what about the Woodstock

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:39.030
concert? Was that the apogee of something?
Was it the beginning of a slide?

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.540
Jim Miller: Well, the way I think that myths
get hatched in the United States is always

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:49.169
a strange mixture of reality and imagination.
As a concert per se, I don’t even — it

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:53.460
wasn’t that important in terms of the large
scale of history of rock and roll. I think

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.259
that the fact that it happened in New York
City, that the media were close to it, magnified

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:02.400
it out of all proportion. And once it had
been so magnified, it became a symbol for

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.960
all kinds of people.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.490
It became an image in the minds of people
who weren’t there of a way that you could

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:15.410
experience music as a cultural lifestyle.
It became a further emblem to the music industry

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:22.040
that there was a market that they had yet
to fully tap. And in the immediate sequel

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.530
to Woodstock, there were numerous attempts
to capture that market.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.949
Ben Wattenberg: You have both stressed a certain
commercial aspect to this.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:31.279
Jim Miller: Yes. And the third thing I would
say is that in the long haul, it has become

00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:36.039
a convenient symbol for people who are hostile
to what the spirit of the ’60s — whatever

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:39.979
that represents — it’s become a convenient
symbol for them and a target.

00:05:39.979 --> 00:05:43.240
Ben Wattenberg: You mean for conservatives,
to be against Woodstock?

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.240
Jim Miller: Yes.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.610
Ben Wattenberg: Morris Dickstein.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:51.210
Morris Dickstein: Well, Woodstock was commercial,
but after all it was a commercial disaster

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.690
that was only recouped by the movie. And it’s
the movie that — as well as some media coverage,

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.840
but mainly the movie, I think, that turned
Woodstock into the enduring myth that it later

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:05.289
became. So many people think they were at
Woodstock, but they really only saw the movie.

00:06:05.289 --> 00:06:06.289
And —

00:06:06.289 --> 00:06:09.659
Ben Wattenberg: That’s like that famous
story of how many people claim they saw Babe

00:06:09.659 --> 00:06:13.240
Ruth point to hitting the home run.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.830
Jim Miller: And how few people voted for Richard
Nixon.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:16.570
Ben Wattenberg: And how few people voted for
— right.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:23.960
Morris Dickstein: But it didn’t really represent
all of the ’60s. It represented those strands

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:27.960
of the counterculture rather than the political
side of the ’60s, but it had its own politics

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.471
because, as your introductory piece said,
it took place in the context of the war. So

00:06:31.471 --> 00:06:38.469
all those days of peace and love amid chaos
and disorganization really was a way of acting

00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:43.069
out a kind of lifestyle protest against the
wars, assassinations, and the whole violent

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:47.460
side of the ’60s that tends to get stressed
more in media stereotypes.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:55.870
Ben Wattenberg: Kurt Vonnegut, you were one
of the cultural icons at that time — like

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:56.870
it or not.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:01.030
Kurt Vonnegut: I never showed myself to my
people. [Laughter.]

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:10.780
I had four kids who were the proper age to
go to Woodstock, and they simply were not

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:17.061
interested, although they were pacifists and
outraged at the government and so forth. They

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:25.009
were attracted to peace marches, to sit-ins,
to teach-ins, and political demonstrations.

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:33.430
And we were talking just in the greenroom
about how much I miss Abbie Hoffman. He was

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:41.449
a great man. He was a useful man for focusing
attention on the outrages, many outrages,

00:07:41.449 --> 00:07:47.830
many injustices and nuttinesses in our society.
And Woodstock did none of that, I guess. As

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.400
you said, it was politically useless.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.020
Martha Bayles: Well, one of the reasons why
there’s this flowery, lovely myth about

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:59.059
Woodstock is because it was, compared to some
of the other things that were going on in

00:07:59.059 --> 00:08:05.330
1969, rather benign. In 1969, SDS had split
into the Weathermen faction —

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:10.319
Ben Wattenberg: The Students for a Democratic
Society, which was the hard left of the student

00:08:10.319 --> 00:08:11.319
movement.

00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:14.629
Martha Bayles: It was the student political
organization. It split into the Weathermen

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:21.189
faction, which was rather — the kind of
terrorist wannabes at that point. I mean,

00:08:21.189 --> 00:08:25.289
they were very hung up on the idea of whether
they could commit violence, I think partly

00:08:25.289 --> 00:08:29.719
because they knew people their own age were
fighting and dying in Vietnam and that was

00:08:29.719 --> 00:08:34.250
a problem for them. And it was partly because
of black movement, which they very much were

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:40.339
copying and emulating — was very much out
of its nonviolent and into its violent stage.

00:08:40.339 --> 00:08:45.530
So Woodstock was a — a lot of kids were
not into that kind of politics, and as a result,

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:47.509
they weren’t into politics especially at
all.

00:08:47.509 --> 00:08:51.149
Morris Dickstein: I think one problem was
that those benign images I think angered a

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:54.361
lot of people in Middle America more than
even the images of protest. I think there

00:08:54.361 --> 00:09:03.709
were some people inspired by images of all
this sex and love and free — especially

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:09.120
the peaceful atmosphere of Woodstock. The
idea of childlike innocence I think inspired

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.290
some people with the desire to kill, you know,
inspired at least other people with — I

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:17.959
think it probably is the ancestor of our culture
wars today. Because what we think of as the

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:23.339
move towards fundamentalism, the New Right
family values, really did emerge as a reaction

00:09:23.339 --> 00:09:25.130
to that countercultural ethos in the ’60s.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.190
Ben Wattenberg: Well, one of the things I
think that comes up when you look at the — it’s

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:32.439
interesting — at the public opinion polls
today is that the American people are afraid

00:09:32.439 --> 00:09:37.399
— many of the American people are afraid
that Bill and Hillary Clinton sort of represent,

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:40.000
quote, “the ’60s” or that part of —

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.540
Morris Dickstein: But Bill Clinton was the
guy running for class president then. He was

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.540
not the guy —

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:48.899
Ben Wattenberg: Yeah, but he was also allegedly
dodging the draft and doing a few other — not

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:52.920
inhaling and a few other things. I mean, you
know, it’s a complex story, obviously.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.560
Martha Bayles: This brings up the third thing
that Woodstock was. It was a rock concert;

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:02.500
it was not a political demonstration. What
it really was, though, on another level, was

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:08.170
that it was something that’s deeply in the
American grain. It was kind of a revival meeting.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:12.680
And there was a quality about Woodstock that
smacked of the outdoor camp meeting, a Methodist

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:13.680
camp meeting.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:20.250
Ben Wattenberg: But why has this hung on as
a symbol? Is that how things work? I mean,

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.529
times get sort of attached to a specific event
and —

00:10:23.529 --> 00:10:30.589
Kurt Vonnegut: I think it represents a very
primitive need in all of us which is seldom

00:10:30.589 --> 00:10:37.000
acknowledged. I think at our peril we do without
vitamin C, for instance. At our peril we do

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:46.310
without a tribe, without a support system.
The nuclear family is not a support system.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:53.959
It’s hideously vulnerable. And so we again
and again join gangs. For most of the seven

00:10:53.959 --> 00:10:58.870
million years there have been human beings,
we have lived in families of 50 or a hundred.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:03.051
Now, the people who talk about family values
— Dan Quayle, for instance, who’s from

00:11:03.051 --> 00:11:10.209
my hometown, he has an extended family. He
might as well be an Ibo in Nigeria. He has

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:12.100
lawyers, cousins, National Guardsmen generals
—

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.110
Martha Bayles: You mean that as a compliment.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:17.779
Kurt Vonnegut: Yes. No, everybody should have
what he has.

00:11:17.779 --> 00:11:18.779
Martha Bayles: We should all be so fortunate.

00:11:18.779 --> 00:11:24.899
Kurt Vonnegut: And when somebody like Quayle
talks about family values, yeah, we’d like

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:26.839
to have families, too. Most of us don’t,
but he’s got one.

00:11:26.839 --> 00:11:30.410
Ben Wattenberg: And in fact, it was called
— I mean, the whole thing was called “the

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.560
movement,” and that was an extended family.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:35.569
Morris Dickstein: The funny thing about that
period, though, is there are so many events

00:11:35.569 --> 00:11:41.490
that mark the period where not just an extended
family, but three, four, 500,000 people got

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:45.060
together. And no matter what happened — whether
it was the music being played that very few

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:49.630
people could hear because of the bad sound
system or whether it was the political demonstration

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.319
in Washington where nobody listened to the
speeches — but just being there was the

00:11:53.319 --> 00:11:56.819
thing. There was a sense of a larger community.

00:11:56.819 --> 00:12:02.209
We may think of it as a very thin idea of
community, but it was a sense of an alienated

00:12:02.209 --> 00:12:05.760
part of the culture, a youth culture that
had been building at least since “Catcher

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:11.589
in the Rye” 20 years earlier, getting together
and asserting itself with its various generational

00:12:11.589 --> 00:12:17.180
anthems. And that was a key element in creating
that sense of a support system that Kurt just

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:18.180
mentioned.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.350
Jim Miller: It strikes me that there’s another
dark side of this —

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:21.350
Martha Bayles: Yes.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:24.300
Jim Miller: — that’s worth bringing into
the picture here, which is just a few days

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:30.410
before Woodstock, it’s worth remembering
“the family” that murdered Sharon Tate,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:38.540
and that Charlie Manson was a version of what
could come out of this kind of pseudo-tribalism

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.129
in a drug-addled atmosphere.

00:12:40.129 --> 00:12:41.129
Martha Bayles: Exactly.

00:12:41.129 --> 00:12:44.899
Jim Miller: And that there’s a way in which
Woodstock — I wonder how the media would

00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:49.519
have played it if they had known at the time,
which they didn’t, that Manson was in his

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:52.470
own way as legitimate a hippie as anybody
who went to Woodstock.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:13:00.149
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask Kurt a question.
I sort of come out of the political arena,

00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:04.420
and in fact I had — during that Vietnam
War, I had worked on President Johnson’s

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:09.379
staff, so we come from rather different backgrounds.

00:13:09.379 --> 00:13:18.139
How representative of American young people
were the people at Woodstock or even the Woodstock

00:13:18.139 --> 00:13:23.139
nation? I mean, I’ll just cite a couple
of poll results from the time that I recall,

00:13:23.139 --> 00:13:29.379
which was that young people were more pro–the
Vietnam War than adults were; young people

00:13:29.379 --> 00:13:36.790
were more pro–George Wallace than adults
were; and in 1972, in that general era, a

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:42.490
majority of young people voted against George
McGovern and for Richard Nixon.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:53.800
So was this typical America, or are we talking
about a thin elite fringe that got the media

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:54.800
attention?

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.060
Kurt Vonnegut: This is a function of education.
It’s ignorant people who —

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.290
Ben Wattenberg: Like me, right? [Laughter.]

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:08.480
Kurt Vonnegut: Who were for the war and so
forth. [Laughs.] No, these were educated people.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:12.720
They were middle class, so I think it could
be attacked on class grounds probably as to

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.720
who they were.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.149
Morris Dickstein: But the counterculture were
middle class also. I mean, there’s a book

00:14:16.149 --> 00:14:19.500
called “Woodstock Nation” that shows that
93 percent of the counterculture were middle

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:22.100
class.

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:27.370
I think the fallacy there is the numbers game.
I mean, there are always a lot of people who

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:30.509
are living the way their parents lived and
the way their grandparents lived. It’s just

00:14:30.509 --> 00:14:39.140
that I think a fairly large part of that generation,
that boomer generation, represented some form

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:43.639
of alienation, whether radical or mild and
so on — really represented a sense of having

00:14:43.639 --> 00:14:47.570
different values from their parents, who were
the World War II generation, or the ’40s

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:51.870
and ’50s generation. And it’s that generation
that tried to assert itself at Woodstock.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:58.209
Ben Wattenberg: Let’s try to move on now
from the event of Woodstock to the consequences

00:14:58.209 --> 00:15:05.240
of Woodstock. And let me see if I can try
it out in a provocative way. What we saw at

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:13.980
Woodstock and from the ’60s, among other
things, was the drug culture, was sexual promiscuity,

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:18.600
and — we could argue about this — was
pacifism. And I would say that if we had remained

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:24.300
a — or became a — pacifist nation, we
would not have won the Cold War, much to the

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:30.790
dismay of people around the world. So what
are the consequences of Woodstock?

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:36.230
Martha Bayles: I think it was a failed project
because a lot of young people believed in

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:41.339
peace and love, but they didn’t have a very
good guide as to what peaceful or loving actions

00:15:41.339 --> 00:15:48.339
really were. And it was very much about personal
liberation and not very much about how to

00:15:48.339 --> 00:15:54.500
treat other people. How to treat other people
became a very confused and chaotic issue for

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:58.050
a lot of young people. Even if they weren’t
at Woodstock or they weren’t at the extremes,

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:02.170
there was a lot of moral confusion. And by
moral confusion, I mean how to treat other

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.540
people.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:09.060
And if you add in the toxic emotions that
are brought about by drug abuse, you have

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:14.390
a recipe for real disaster. And people who
felt mistreated by their friends and their

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:19.459
lovers and so forth in the ’60s were always
accused of having middle-class hang-ups, you

00:16:19.459 --> 00:16:23.540
know. Hey, you know, what’s your problem?
Why do you object to what I just did to you?

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.600
You know, you’re just hung up on middle-class
morality.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:30.800
And that affected a lot of people. I don’t
know how the polls would measure that, but

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.000
that affected a lot of people.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:37.160
Ben Wattenberg: Kurt, are we a better country
because of the ’60s today?

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:45.370
Kurt Vonnegut: Yes, I think so. The ’60s
taught us that we can build an extended family

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:54.749
and unify ourselves with music. Feminism did
not come out of the ’60s. I’ve talked

00:16:54.749 --> 00:16:59.999
to any number of women who were the right
age to be involved, who were treated abominably,

00:16:59.999 --> 00:17:02.550
as slaves, as gofers while the men —

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.490
Martha Bayles: Well, I sum it up this way.
In the ’60s, men liberated themselves from

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:11.470
the demands made on them traditionally by
women. In the ’70s, women liberated themselves

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:13.919
from the demands traditionally made by men.
And the —

00:17:13.919 --> 00:17:18.199
Morris Dickstein: On the same model that the
men had used and the blacks had used and so

00:17:18.199 --> 00:17:19.199
on.

00:17:19.199 --> 00:17:20.319
Martha Bayles: And the tragedy is that they
both liberated themselves from the demands

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:21.319
made by children.

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:25.220
Kurt Vonnegut: I would say that the big split
is between the people with senses of humor

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:29.490
and people who are humorless. [Laughter.]
As you know —

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.160
Martha Bayles: That’s always a problem.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:36.210
Kurt Vonnegut: Yes. It all has to go on this
way, you know, and it’s real-world politicking.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:44.611
And we have this example of a country being
able to go absolutely nuts in Germany. And

00:17:44.611 --> 00:17:50.260
so we could start that way with these military
adventures and —

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:51.840
Ben Wattenberg: Are you talking about now?

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:57.240
Kurt Vonnegut: No, no, no. I’m talking about
the Vietnam War, which was finally — it

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.540
had to be brought to an end somehow.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:04.460
Morris Dickstein: I would describe it a little
differently. Woodstock represented a failed

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:09.580
utopianism that very easily got commercialized
and I think very easily got turned into style.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:14.789
You know, what happened — I mean, the people
who were in their own way protesting the war

00:18:14.789 --> 00:18:21.159
and other things at Woodstock were acting
out a criticism, not by going to the ballot

00:18:21.159 --> 00:18:26.970
box, but by the way they dressed, the clothes
they wore, various kinds of mores that got

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:31.820
the label “lifestyle” later on. And the
counterculture — unlike the New Left, the

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:35.650
counterculture was very amenable to being
turned into something that could easily be

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:36.650
commercialized.

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:40.200
Jim Miller: I think I really disagree with
you. I think actually I would put it almost

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:45.570
the other way around, that the New Left, which
I’ve written a book about, I think ultimately

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:50.551
isn’t that important politically, and it
didn’t have that great an impact, except

00:18:50.551 --> 00:18:53.960
indirectly by sparking a middle-class peace
movement.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:19:00.200
The counterculture, precisely because it was
picked up and shoved into the marketplace,

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:05.710
spread the ethos of the ’60s in the early
’70s, and in the process of what you’re,

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:09.799
I think, denigrating as style or fashion,
actually changed the culture and changed it

00:19:09.799 --> 00:19:10.799
in ways —

00:19:10.799 --> 00:19:12.070
Ben Wattenberg: Changed, for example, our
sexual mores.

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:19.059
Jim Miller: It changed sexual mores. It changed
attitudes towards authority. It changed a

00:19:19.059 --> 00:19:25.310
sense of what was permissible to experiment
with, what limits could be toyed with. What

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:30.900
drove them home in American culture was the
marketing through rock and roll, among other

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:39.210
things, through film, of a certain fantasy
of freedom that to me has connections, say,

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:43.700
with Randolph Borne, early progressive Bohemians.
But it becomes a mass artifact.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.380
Ben Wattenberg: Martha, what did it do to
our music?

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:51.390
Martha Bayles: Well, I think — I agree with
Jim — I think that our commercial system

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:57.090
has the ability to amplify, magnify and multiply
and popularize any cultural change, and this

00:19:57.090 --> 00:20:01.220
does make the counterculture different from
previous forms of Bohemianism. It because

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.289
a mass phenomenon, which Morris has written
about also.

00:20:04.289 --> 00:20:06.980
Morris Dickstein: But it’s not just the
marketing. I think what happened is that — it’s

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:11.140
in that original reaction to Woodstock and
the counterculture — Middle America looked

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:14.990
at that with a combination of hostility and
envy.

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:16.450
Martha Bayles: Not young Middle America.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:20.730
Morris Dickstein: Well, no, I think they did.
I think that in one sense that traditional

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:26.990
values were threatened by the new mores represented
by the counterculture; in another sense they

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:30.961
thought, God, I wish I were young now, or
I wish I were involved in this. There was

00:20:30.961 --> 00:20:36.300
a kind of a deep sexual fantasy and fantasy
of freedom involved. That was part of the

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:37.360
marketing and so on.

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:41.130
And what happened is that gradually this was
shorn of any political context it might have

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:45.380
had and entirely personalized and turned into
the kind of transformation of cultural mores

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:46.380
that you describe.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:51.110
Ben Wattenberg: Let me return to that political
context for a moment. The component parts

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:58.750
of “the movement” — one could say it
started with civil rights and that it accumulated

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.120
feminism and environmentalism and consumerism
and —

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.590
Martha Bayles: No. The Vietnam War would be
the —

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:08.649
Ben Wattenberg: And the peace movement. Who
would accept here the idea that there was

00:21:08.649 --> 00:21:12.279
some good in each one of those movements?

00:21:12.279 --> 00:21:13.809
Martha Bayles: I would.

00:21:13.809 --> 00:21:15.190
Morris Dickstein: We all would.

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:19.169
Ben Wattenberg: Wait a minute, wait a minute
— but they each went too far?

00:21:19.169 --> 00:21:23.390
Morris Dickstein: Everything that makes an
impact goes too far. In other words, things

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:28.130
that are moderate and easy and so on do not
really make an impact. What happens is that

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:33.040
there’s some transformational push, and
eventually a lot of it drops away and doesn’t

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:37.460
really have its effect. But some of it sticks
and becomes part of this permanent cultural

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.679
change that Jim was describing. I think every
one of those movements that you described

00:21:41.679 --> 00:21:42.679
had that effect.

00:21:42.679 --> 00:21:46.620
Ben Wattenberg: Kurt, did it go too far? Did
these movements go too far?

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:53.020
Kurt Vonnegut: No. I would like to see them
all, so many of them go much farther —environmentalism,

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:58.820
for instance, which you mentioned.

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:04.770
And so I think you’re right, a movement
is a failure, really, if it doesn’t go too

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:05.770
far. [Laughs.]

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:09.710
Martha Bayles: If I look back on what I think
is good about the ’60s from my own perspective,

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:15.630
that of a white person with a privileged background,
okay, one of the nice things about the ’60s

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:19.289
and positive things about the ’60s was it
encouraged you to pay serious attention to

00:22:19.289 --> 00:22:22.560
what life was like for the people on the other
side of the tracks.

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:26.910
The way it went too far was that a lot of
the radical political part of the movement

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:32.470
had fantasies about those people and never
really understood them very well. And particularly

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:39.090
with blacks, this incredible fantasy of blacks
as violent and super alienated, this Panther

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:42.760
fantasy that the left had, I think has been
extremely destructive.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:49.309
Ben Wattenberg: Try to sum up for us and for
our audience. What do you all agree upon,

00:22:49.309 --> 00:22:50.650
and what do you disagree upon?

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:53.880
Morris Dickstein: I think everything that
we look back to the ’60s and think about,

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:59.890
including the counterculture, has its upside,
its positive side in terms of the liberation,

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:06.039
the easing, the relaxing of American mores
that many of us grew up with in the 1950s,

00:23:06.039 --> 00:23:11.850
and also had its side in which it went too
far — in other words, the sense that it

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:15.570
unleashed all sorts of demons that we see
today in the culture wars.

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:19.070
Ben Wattenberg: Kurt, what do we agree upon?
What do we disagree upon?

00:23:19.070 --> 00:23:25.630
Kurt Vonnegut: Well, I think that probably
an important movement was going on invisibly

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:33.110
in the universities, in the coffee houses,
among intelligent people prepared to do political

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:37.140
work. And so concerts really had nothing to
do with it.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:45.049
But it seems after the fact, an awful lot
of important, devoted thinking has come out

00:23:45.049 --> 00:23:53.340
of the ’60s relative to rescuing the planet,
to questioning authority on military adventures,

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.059
and so forth. And so we —

00:23:55.059 --> 00:23:57.250
Ben Wattenberg: As we prepare to invade Haiti.

00:23:57.250 --> 00:23:59.340
Kurt Vonnegut: Yes. I’m ready. I’m going.

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:02.580
Morris Dickstein: There is a skepticism that
can’t be put aside.

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:09.110
Kurt Vonnegut: Yeah, but the — I think it
happened in the souls of many of us, and of

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:10.200
course those are invisible.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.200
Ben Wattenberg: Jim.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:18.010
Jim Miller: Above all else, an experiment
in living that was shared and collective — not

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:24.760
shared by everybody. Like a real experiment,
we learn through mistakes, but to speak just

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:26.740
for myself, I would do it all over again.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:29.630
Ben Wattenberg: Martha, you’ve got the last
shot.

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:36.700
Martha Bayles: I think we agree there were
angels at Woodstock and also devils. And we

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:40.100
may not — we may disagree somewhat about
which was which.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:46.880
Ben Wattenberg: All right. Thank you, Martha
Bayles, Jim Miller, Morris Dickstein, and

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.400
Kurt Vonnegut.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:54.289
And thank you. We are very happy to hear from
our viewers. Please send any comments or questions

00:24:54.289 --> 00:24:56.280
to the address on the screen.

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.340
For “Think Tank,” I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:27.540
Announcer: This has been a production of BJW
